WEBVTT

00:03.000 --> 00:10.440
The Power of Source Code

00:10.440 --> 00:19.540
Yeah, hi everybody, it's Source
Code. It is Saturday, August
21st, 2004. I'm Adam Curry.

00:19.540 --> 00:27.240
Saturday afternoon in Curry
Castle in Belgium. It's about
3.36. How's that for an
approximate time for you?

00:27.240 --> 00:35.360
Sorry about yesterday. No
postings on the blog and no
source code. It was just way too
crazy.

00:35.360 --> 00:39.760
During the radio show in the
morning, as you know, we're
winding down.

00:40.160 --> 00:44.500
One more week to go and then two
more days to finish up the month
and that'll be all over.

00:44.500 --> 00:50.920
We had the Chanuk crew who flew
us in Iraq.

00:50.920 --> 01:03.020
They flew us from Camp Smitty to
Basra.

01:04.500 --> 01:07.620
a final two or three hour drive
from there to Kuwait on our way
home.

01:07.620 --> 01:12.080
And we also did some night
goggle vision reconnaissance
flights.

01:12.080 --> 01:13.300
So anyway, they came by.

01:13.300 --> 01:17.400
They wanted to see where we
worked since we'd already seen
their area of responsibility.

01:17.400 --> 01:20.300
It was a good time, but tiring
nonetheless.

01:20.300 --> 01:22.700
From there, I went straight
home.

01:22.700 --> 01:29.940
There was someone who almost
arrived immediately for an
interview, which was, of course,
all

01:29.940 --> 01:30.300
set up.

01:30.300 --> 01:34.100
But I keep forgetting I really
don't like doing stuff on Friday
afternoons.

01:34.960 --> 01:41.120
And then on top of that, some
folks who had seen the house
earlier in the week came back,

01:41.120 --> 01:41.920
wanted to see it again.

01:41.920 --> 01:43.520
This time they brought the
entire family.

01:43.520 --> 01:49.420
And I got a call earlier this
morning that they are coming
back again on Monday.

01:49.420 --> 01:50.680
So that looks good.

01:52.500 --> 02:03.780
I don't know if you remember,
two days ago I mentioned that
Crystal, my assistant, had...

02:03.780 --> 02:06.760
I'm not quite sure where she got
it from.

02:06.760 --> 02:16.460
She got one of these Pinnacle
Systems Show Center, which is
essentially a video device that
you hook up to your television.

02:16.460 --> 02:18.840
I have it hooked up to our big
Grandmaster View screen here.

02:19.660 --> 02:23.980
It has a Wi-Fi card, so it talks
to your Wi-Fi environment.

02:23.980 --> 02:32.860
And it sits there basically
listening for a show center
server, which I'm not quite sure
what protocol it speaks.

02:32.860 --> 02:35.500
It may be Windows Media Server,
I'm not sure.

02:35.500 --> 02:42.100
But that has to run on a Windows
machine, so I still have an old
Sony VAIO laptop with Windows
2000.

02:42.100 --> 02:45.840
Loaded it up, and lo and behold,
it's working.

02:45.840 --> 02:49.500
First of all, regarding the...

02:49.500 --> 02:50.040
the setup

02:50.040 --> 02:51.420
the wifi setup

02:51.420 --> 02:52.000
is stupid

02:52.000 --> 02:53.060
you have to fill in

02:54.000 --> 03:00.420
name, a network name, and it
comes with a show center as the
network name installed

03:00.420 --> 03:03.280
standard, which makes no sense
because, of course, that means
everyone's going to have

03:03.280 --> 03:03.960
to change that.

03:03.960 --> 03:08.760
Either you have to change your
Wi-Fi network name or you have
to figure out how to change

03:08.760 --> 03:15.280
it on the show center device,
which means you've got to
reboot, hold down the B key.

03:15.280 --> 03:17.940
I mean, this is all stuff that
is just not easy for people.

03:17.940 --> 03:19.740
I think it should work like my
laptop does.

03:19.740 --> 03:24.640
When I walk in range of a new
Wi-Fi network, it says, hey,
should I add this?

03:24.640 --> 03:25.720
Do you want to connect to it
now?

03:25.720 --> 03:28.140
That, of course, is the way this
should work as well.

03:28.140 --> 03:33.840
So basically, you create a
database in the show center
software on the PC.

03:33.840 --> 03:41.680
Of course, just like a share
program, like a peer-to-peer
program, if you want, it'll

03:41.680 --> 03:45.960
go through your entire disk and
pull up all kinds of media
files.

03:45.960 --> 03:48.360
So that works pretty
straightforward.

03:48.360 --> 03:49.360
And it's basically...

03:49.360 --> 03:52.240
Basically, you have three bins,
music, movies, photos.

03:52.240 --> 03:53.960
You can create playlists from
them.

03:55.500 --> 03:58.160
is the files that it will
accept.

03:58.160 --> 04:00.800
Let me see.

04:00.800 --> 04:01.800
I think I have...

04:01.800 --> 04:06.980
Here, it does tell me which
files it'll accept.

04:06.980 --> 04:08.760
Hold on.

04:10.500 --> 04:23.840
mp3 windows media audio wave
file jpeg png gif mpeg1 mpeg2
divx avi version 5 plus 4 plus 5

04:23.840 --> 04:33.540
simple profile remember that is
x video avi simple profile dv
avi windows media video video
files

04:33.540 --> 04:39.080
also must all contain audio data
well so apparently there's a
quite a difference between

04:39.080 --> 04:46.860
avis and avis because some
really i had this huge bucket of
bit torrent downloads which

04:46.860 --> 04:53.740
are either mpegs or avis and
some of the avis do work on the
show center if you put it in a

04:53.740 --> 05:03.160
windows media video it converts
it to a vcd file you know so
it's a little bit disappointing
that

05:03.160 --> 05:08.700
you just don't know exactly what
is going to work or not and if
you should convert prior to

05:08.700 --> 05:10.260
putting it into this database

05:10.260 --> 05:12.000
or if it

05:12.000 --> 05:13.720
will do it by itself. It's very
unclear.

05:13.720 --> 05:17.960
Something it does have that's
pretty cool is you can

05:17.960 --> 05:19.820
set up a folder to be watched

05:19.820 --> 05:21.860
and the minute you drop

05:21.860 --> 05:23.840
anything into that folder,
automatically

05:23.840 --> 05:26.080
it will be added to the database
and then

05:26.080 --> 05:27.900
from the control center, the
show center

05:27.900 --> 05:29.740
in the living room, which has a
remote control,

05:29.740 --> 05:31.920
makes it nice, you can already

05:31.920 --> 05:34.100
a little light pops on saying
new media

05:34.100 --> 05:35.820
and you go to the new media menu

05:35.820 --> 05:37.540
it will show up and it will
play.

05:37.540 --> 05:39.900
Essentially when you select new
media to be played

05:39.900 --> 05:41.560
it's being streamed across

05:41.560 --> 05:43.020
my Wi-Fi network.

05:43.020 --> 05:45.140
So I'm going to play with it a
little bit more

05:45.140 --> 05:47.740
and I'm interested to see how

05:47.740 --> 05:50.060
Tricia, my wife, and Christina,
our daughter

05:50.060 --> 05:51.060
how they pick it up

05:51.060 --> 05:54.020
and how they use it and I'm
putting mounted

05:54.020 --> 05:55.940
volumes on their

05:55.940 --> 05:57.840
computer desktops so they can
just

05:57.840 --> 05:59.500
drag and drop stuff in there as
well.

05:59.500 --> 06:01.580
So we'll see what happens. It'll
be cool.

06:01.580 --> 06:06.880
Right.

06:06.880 --> 06:09.860
I told you that I've been
reading

06:09.860 --> 06:11.120
...

06:11.120 --> 06:13.500
That would be big quotation
marks.

06:13.500 --> 06:15.740
Michael Crichton's Timeline.

06:15.740 --> 06:17.880
The reason why I say reading

06:17.880 --> 06:19.740
is because I've been listening
to it on audiobook.

06:19.740 --> 06:22.260
I actually just finished it
yesterday afternoon

06:22.260 --> 06:24.260
on my drive home, sitting in
some extra traffic.

06:24.260 --> 06:28.020
So I even was able to finish up

06:28.020 --> 06:29.440
the last 25 minutes I thought

06:29.440 --> 06:31.760
I wouldn't be able to listen to
during my drive home.

06:31.760 --> 06:34.060
Great book.

06:34.060 --> 06:35.000
Really enjoyed it.

06:35.000 --> 06:37.260
14 hours worth of audio.

06:37.260 --> 06:40.020
And I got an email from Dave
Weiner.

06:40.020 --> 06:41.420
Let me just pick that up here.

06:41.420 --> 06:46.440
FYI, I've become a member of
audible.com

06:46.440 --> 06:47.780
and am reading

06:47.780 --> 06:49.520
Timeline now as I drive.

06:49.520 --> 06:50.620
He's driving across country.

06:50.620 --> 06:53.480
It's a very nice, if somewhat
paranoid, service.

06:53.480 --> 06:55.880
I guess he's referring to...

06:55.880 --> 06:58.380
He could be referring to the
book or to audible.com.

06:58.380 --> 07:00.200
Thanks for the tip in your audio
blog post.

07:00.200 --> 07:02.220
Next stop, St. Augustine.

07:02.220 --> 07:02.780
Dave.

07:02.780 --> 07:03.220
Okay.

07:03.220 --> 07:06.460
So there's something
interesting.

07:06.460 --> 07:08.560
I was talking about the
audiobook.

07:08.560 --> 07:11.400
I think what I even said is...

07:11.400 --> 07:15.000
Gee, wouldn't it be cool if I
could attach one of those DRM

07:15.000 --> 07:21.860
protected audio files, because
Audible.com uses the AAC, Apple,
DRM, can't copy me stuff.

07:21.860 --> 07:26.160
And if you tried to open it in
iTunes just by double clicking
it, it would then say, hey,

07:26.160 --> 07:31.240
you know, you want to buy this
book? Looks like Adam referred
it to you. Of course, they have
a

07:31.240 --> 07:38.340
number, it's DRM, so they know
that I bought it. And maybe they
could give me two bucks for
selling

07:38.340 --> 07:44.040
another copy, because I think it
goes for, I don't know, it goes
for at least $10. What I want to
do

07:44.040 --> 07:49.880
today, though, is share with you
a little piece from that book, a
monologue, which I found very

07:49.880 --> 07:59.840
interesting and very applicable
to perhaps the reason why we
have weblogs today. Now, if
you're

07:59.840 --> 08:05.420
reading this book still and
don't want to hear any parts of
it, then you should probably not
listen

08:05.420 --> 08:11.900
to this. It's definitely not a
spoiler, doesn't mess you up at
all about the plot or the
outcome

08:11.900 --> 08:14.020
of the book. But it's a
monologue by one of my friends,
and I'm going to share with you
a little

08:14.020 --> 08:16.500
one of the characters and he's
basically doing

08:16.500 --> 08:24.860
a pitch for this vision that he
has, which is irrelevant, but
listen to the theory behind

08:24.860 --> 08:25.300
the pitch.

08:25.300 --> 08:30.380
Now, of course, since this is an
AAC file, I can't open it in any
other application but

08:30.380 --> 08:30.840
iTunes.

08:30.840 --> 08:33.080
I tried to open it in QuickTime,
didn't work either.

08:33.080 --> 08:38.260
So I can't open it into my
recording application, so I'm
going to have to put the mic
down on

08:38.260 --> 08:42.760
the speaker so that I can start
up iTunes and you can listen to
this.

08:42.760 --> 08:45.540
This is about three minutes, but
it's a very interesting thing.

08:45.540 --> 08:47.040
Hi.

08:54.000 --> 08:56.560
He walked back and forth across
the stage.

08:56.560 --> 09:01.640
Today, everybody expects to be
entertained, and they expect to
be entertained all the time.

09:01.640 --> 09:08.140
Business meetings must be
snappy, with bullet lists and
animated graphics, so executives
aren't bored.

09:08.140 --> 09:13.240
Malls and stores must be
engaging, so they amuse as well
as sell us.

09:13.240 --> 09:18.680
Politicians must have pleasing
video personalities and tell us
only what we want to hear.

09:19.000 --> 09:24.580
Schools must be careful not to
bore young minds that expect the
speed and complexity of
television.

09:24.580 --> 09:29.320
Students must be amused,
everyone must be amused, or they
will switch.

09:29.320 --> 09:32.780
Switch brands, switch channels,
switch parties, switch
loyalties.

09:32.780 --> 09:38.360
This is the intellectual reality
of Western society at the end of
the century.

09:38.360 --> 09:44.960
In other centuries, human beings
wanted to be saved, or improved,
or freed, or educated.

09:44.960 --> 09:47.560
But in our century, they want to
be entertained.

09:48.340 --> 09:52.680
The great fear is not of disease
or death, but of boredom.

09:52.680 --> 09:55.440
A sense of time on our hands.

09:55.500 --> 10:00.000
a sense of nothing to do, a
sense that we are not amused.

10:00.000 --> 10:03.800
But where will this mania for
entertainment end?

10:03.800 --> 10:09.240
What will people do when they
get tired of television, when
they get tired of movies?

10:09.240 --> 10:11.180
We already know the answer.

10:11.180 --> 10:17.700
They go into participatory
activities, sports, theme parks,
amusement rides, roller
coasters,

10:17.700 --> 10:21.660
structured fun, planned thrills.

10:21.660 --> 10:26.280
And what will they do when they
tire of theme parks and planned
thrills?

10:26.280 --> 10:30.380
Sooner or later, the artifice
becomes too noticeable.

10:30.380 --> 10:37.880
They begin to realize that an
amusement park is really a kind
of jail in which you pay to be
an inmate.

10:37.880 --> 10:41.860
This artifice will drive them to
seek authenticity.

10:41.860 --> 10:47.580
Authenticity will be the
buzzword of the 21st century.

10:47.580 --> 10:50.120
And what is authentic?

10:50.120 --> 10:51.600
Anything.

10:51.600 --> 10:54.520
Anything that is not devised and
structured to make a profit.

10:54.520 --> 10:57.000
Anything that is not controlled
by control.

10:57.000 --> 11:04.760
corporations, anything that
exists for its own sake, that
assumes its own shape. But of
course,

11:04.760 --> 11:10.820
nothing in the modern world is
allowed to assume its own shape.
The modern world is the
corporate

11:10.820 --> 11:17.740
equivalent of a formal garden,
where everything is planted and
arranged for effect, where
nothing is

11:17.740 --> 11:27.440
untouched, where nothing is
authentic. Where, then, will
people turn for the rare and
desirable

11:27.440 --> 11:38.460
experience of authenticity? They
will turn to the past. The past
is unarguably authentic.

11:38.460 --> 11:47.500
The past is a world that already
existed before Disney and
Murdoch and Nissan and Sony and
IBM

11:47.500 --> 11:47.720
and Amazon.

11:47.740 --> 11:55.000
All the other shapers of the
present day. The past was here
before they were. The past rose

11:55.000 --> 11:58.500
and fell without their intrusion
and molding and

11:58.500 --> 12:00.640
selling. The past is

12:00.640 --> 12:02.980
real. It's authentic.

12:02.980 --> 12:04.560
And this will

12:04.560 --> 12:06.560
make the past unbelievably

12:06.560 --> 12:08.180
attractive.

12:08.180 --> 12:10.280
Well, that's

12:10.280 --> 12:11.820
probably about enough of that.

12:11.820 --> 12:14.840
And hopefully it sounded okay
through my

12:14.840 --> 12:18.660
air-based audio bridge.

12:18.660 --> 12:20.540
So I thought that was just an

12:20.540 --> 12:22.040
interesting bit of monologue.

12:22.040 --> 12:24.560
One about how people were going
to look for

12:24.560 --> 12:26.860
reality. And maybe that's what
weblogs

12:26.860 --> 12:28.640
bring if you subscribe to that

12:28.640 --> 12:30.840
type of vision. That weblogs

12:30.840 --> 12:32.520
bring a sense of reality. Or

12:32.520 --> 12:34.720
multiple weblogs can triangulate

12:34.720 --> 12:36.600
reality. And the whole

12:36.600 --> 12:38.160
bit about the past, who knows?

12:38.160 --> 12:40.380
Maybe that's what archives are
all about.

12:40.380 --> 12:43.560
So what else?

12:43.560 --> 12:44.680
um...

12:46.500 --> 12:52.040
iPodder update, the application
that turns your iPod into an
audio MP3 aggregator.

12:52.040 --> 12:56.380
So the current status is you
